window.docContents[103].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.State/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___State":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E › State.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 183 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\nDeclarations on this page (21)abbrev Bytesdef spqrOffBstructure EpochGhostdef ofGhostdef senddef recvstructure BinStatedef lenFielddef bytesFielddef uint32Fielddef msgFielddef encChainKeydef encMessageKeydef encSenderChaindef encReceiverChaindef placeholderBaseKeydef encPlaceholderPendingPreKeydef encSessionStructureSeeddef encodeRecorddef binStateOfdef shortCiphertext\n\n\n\nBinary Party State and Session-Record Encoding (BinState, encodeRecord)Trusted: the statement. Defines the binary-level party state BinState (serialized SessionRecord bytes paired with ACD19 epoch bookkeeping EpochGhost, DEV-M12), the SPQR-disabled ratchet instance spqrOffB (DEV-LS24), the storage.proto encoder encodeRecord (with constant pending pre-key fields in SM mode, DEV-M9), the state map binStateOf, and the < 2^32-byte ciphertext length bound shortCiphertext (DEV-M1). See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\nabbrev BytesL33–34 ↗#Byte strings.\n\ndef spqrOffBL36–44 ↗#SPQR switched off at PQ = Bytes: initial state [], send returns the unchanged empty state, no salt and an empty message for any coins, and recv is what the in-image spqr::recv does on the empty state (LibsignalSpqr.spqrRecvEmpty): [] and no salt if the message's preamble parses (msg_preamble), else Err(MsgDecode) (DEV-LS24). spqrOff (PQ = Unit, Computational/Proofs/SignalWireRoundTrip.lean) is the same except that its recv accepts every message.\n\nThe binary state\n\nstructure EpochGhostL48–55 ↗#The epoch bookkeeping of a party: its epoch counter and, oldest first, the ratchet public key and epoch of each receiver chain its record stores.\n\n\n\ndef ofGhostL59–62 ↗#The epoch bookkeeping of a session-model state: its epoch counter, and its receiver chains' ratchet keys paired with their epochs.\n\ndef sendL64–67 ↗#After a successful send by P (ACD19 Fig. 2 ep-mgmt): the epoch moves to P's next sending epoch if it is in a receiving one (as the session model's Params.send).\n\ndef recvL69–79 ↗#After a successful receive of a message with ratchet key rk: the epoch t of the chain used and the new bookkeeping. A known key keeps everything; an unknown key starts epoch epoch + 1, which becomes the party's epoch, and its chain is appended (libsignal then keeps the last maxReceiverChains chains, session.rs add_receiver_chain), as the session model's Params.receive. ACD19 §5.2 \"Epoch indexing\": Signal names epochs by ratchet keys (DEV-LS8).\n\n\n\nstructure BinStateL83–97 ↗#A party's state in the binary scheme: the party, its serialized session record (the bytes the binary returned), and its epoch bookkeeping.DEV-M12 (formalization): the state carries bookkeeping the binary does not hold. ACD19 Fig. 8 keeps an explicit epoch counter t_cur in the state. The binary's record has none: Signal names epochs by ratchet keys (ACD19 §5.2). Lean adds the party and the epochs next to the record bytes; corr also leaks them. Effect: none: they are public bookkeeping.\n\nField writers (prost encode of one field)\n\ndef lenFieldL101–103 ↗#A length-delimited field: key, length, payload.\n\ndef bytesFieldL105–107 ↗#proto3 bytes: omitted when empty.\n\ndef uint32FieldL109–111 ↗#proto3 uint32: omitted when 0.\n\ndef msgFieldL113–116 ↗#A sub-message: written whenever present (even if its encoding is empty).\n\nMessages of storage.proto\n\ndef encChainKeyL120–122 ↗#Chain.ChainKey { uint32 index = 1; bytes key = 2; } (the key's 32 little-endian bytes).\n\ndef encMessageKeyL124–126 ↗#Chain.MessageKey in its Seed form (the only form the session model has): index = 1, seed = 5.\n\ndef encSenderChainL128–131 ↗#Chain for the sending chain: public key, private key, chain key, no message keys.\n\ndef encReceiverChainL133–136 ↗#Chain for a receiving chain: public key, chain key, skipped message keys (oldest first).\n\ndef placeholderBaseKeyL138–140 ↗#The placeholder base key of Alice's pending pre-key in encodeRecord: the all-zero X25519 key, serialized 0x05 ‖ 0^32 (DEV-M9).\n\ndef encPlaceholderPendingPreKeyL142–145 ↗#PendingPreKey { optional uint32 pre_key_id = 1; bytes base_key = 2; int32 signed_pre_key_id = 3; uint64 timestamp = 4; } at constant values: pre_key_id absent, signed_pre_key_id and timestamp 0 (omitted), so only base_key.\n\ndef encSessionStructureSeedL147–158 ↗#SessionStructure of a session-model state, fields in tag order 1–7, 9, 15 (10, 11, 13, 14 are the omitted defaults: registration ids 0, empty alice_base_key, no Kyber pre-key).\n\ndef encodeRecordL160–163 ↗#SessionRecord::serialize of a session-model state: RecordStructure { current_session = 1; } (no previous sessions).\n\nThe map to binary states, and the injection filter\n\ndef binStateOfL167–184 ↗#binStateOf: the binary state of a session-model state: the same party, encodeRecord, the epoch bookkeeping. Used for the CLI scheme's initial states (cliScheme) and to relabel adversaries (Adversary.relabel).\n\ndef shortCiphertextL173–181 ↗#shortCiphertext: the injections the CLI games admit: shorter than 2^32 bytes (cliPqLenOk, Computational/Specifications/Game/StatementPq.lean). The relabelled adversary answers the others none, as the games do.DEV-M1 (machine model): inputs of 2^32 bytes or more are refused. ACD19 Fig. 2 lets the adversary inject any ciphertext. Here the games refuse such injections, and the CLI specs say nothing about them. Why: the run model needs bounded lengths. Effect: restricts the adversary (no claim for injections of 4 GiB or more); honest ciphertexts are shorter.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/LibsignalE2E/State.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.LibsignalE2E.State/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___LibsignalE2E___State"}});